INT. CHEESE-AGING CAVE - NIGHT

A limestone cavern carved into a mountainside, lined with wooden racks holding ten thousand wheels of alpine cheese. Humidity drips from the ceiling. The air is thick as a wet wool coat. A chain-link door stands at the far end, a keypad glowing red.

SABINE LAROCHE, forties, a former pastry chef turned safecracker, wears a hairnet and a white lab coat. She kneels at the keypad with a screwdriver in her teeth.

MOSES TREVELYAN, fifties, a tall, stooped man who once forged museum labels, stands lookout in rubber boots. He holds a flashlight and a wheel of Gruyere like a baby.

GINO BELLAMY, thirties, a nervous driver in a hooded sweatshirt, paces behind them, whispering into a walkie-talkie.

GINO
Sabine, the guard changes at three. It's two fifty.

SABINE
(through the screwdriver)
I know what time it is.

MOSES
Please do not breathe on the Gruyere. It's eighteen months old and extremely sensitive.

GINO
Moses, you are literally holding it.

MOSES
That's different. I love it.

Sabine pulls the keypad cover off. Wires spill out like a nest. She selects a pair and strips them with her teeth.

SABINE
Quiet. Both of you.

GINO
What if it trips an alarm?

MOSES
It will not trip an alarm.

SABINE
It might trip an alarm.

MOSES
It will not, Sabine, I studied the schematic.

GINO
You studied the schematic of a cheese cave?

MOSES
It's a very serious cheese cave.

She twists the wires together. A green light blinks. The door clicks.

GINO
Oh my God.

SABINE
Don't say it.

GINO
It opened.

MOSES
Do not say anything!

The door swings wide. Beyond it, a steel vault shaped like a giant wheel of cheese, the combination dial glittering.

MOSES (CONT'D)
(reverent)
There she is.

Gino presses his walkie to his ear and his face drains.

GINO
Guys. Guys, guard's early. He's coming down the tunnel.

SABINE
How early?

GINO
Now.

Footsteps echo. A distant voice, bored, singing a folk song. Sabine rises, wipes her hands. Moses sets down the Gruyere and takes up a ladle from a bucket. Gino drops behind a rack.

MOSES
He'll check the door.

SABINE
Then we close the door.

She yanks it shut. The latch snaps and the keypad goes dark, plunging the cavern into blackness except for Moses's flashlight beam shaking on the wall.

GINO
(whisper)
Is it locked?

MOSES
(whisper)
From which side?

Sabine stares at the door, at the dead keypad.

SABINE
Oh no.

GINO
What oh no? What "oh no?"

SABINE
I locked us inside.

The singing stops. A flashlight beam sweeps along the racks toward them.

GINO
I'll talk to him.

MOSES
You'll do no such thing.

SABINE
Both of you, shut up, I'm thinking.

GINO
We're thieves in a cheese cave.

MOSES
We are gourmets in a cheese cave.

SABINE
Quiet!

She snatches the screwdriver, jams it in the latch, twists. Metal shrieks. The door pops open an inch, then two, then flies wide.

The guard's beam hits the vault.

Sabine slams the door shut again from the inside and holds it, her whole body against the steel. Moses and Gino pile in beside her. The three of them pant in the dark.

GUARD (O.S.)
(muffled, cheerful)
Evening, folks!

Nobody breathes.

MOSES
(a whisper)
He's gone past.

GINO
How do you know?

MOSES
He's still singing.

Faintly, the song resumes and fades down the tunnel. Sabine slides to the floor. Gino laughs, high and helpless. Moses retrieves the Gruyere and kisses it.

SABINE
The vault. Now. Before I lose my mind.

She turns the dial. Click, click, click. The great wheel door groans open, and inside, stacked to the ceiling, are not diamonds, not bearer bonds, but ten thousand more wheels of cheese.

GINO
(flat)
It's cheese.

MOSES
(gently)
It's the cheese.

SABINE
(a long beat)
We were robbing the wrong cave.
